\section*{Transmission Gate}
A transmission gate is a bidirectional switch made up of a parallel combination of an nMOS and a pMOS transistor. 
The nMOS transistor passes strong logic ‘0’ but weak logic ‘1’, while the pMOS passes strong logic ‘1’ but weak logic ‘0’. 
By combining both transistors in parallel and driving them with complementary control signals, the transmission gate can pass both logic levels efficiently.

When the control input (EN) is high, the nMOS is ON and the pMOS is also ON (driven by the complement of EN), thus forming a closed switch between input and output. 
When EN is low, both transistors are OFF, forming an open circuit. 
Transmission gates are widely used in multiplexers, latches, and bus systems due to their low propagation delay and small area.
