\chapter{Theory and Working Principle}
\section*{Implementation of 4:1 Multiplexer Using 2:1 Multiplexers}

A 4:1 multiplexer can be constructed by interconnecting three 2:1 multiplexers in a hierarchical structure. 
The 4:1 MUX has four data inputs (\( I_0, I_1, I_2, I_3 \)), two selection inputs (\( S_1, S_0 \)), and one output (\( Y \)).

The basic concept is to first select one input from each pair using two 2:1 multiplexers and then use a third 2:1 multiplexer to select between their outputs.

\begin{itemize}
    \item The first 2:1 MUX (MUX1) selects between \( I_0 \) and \( I_1 \) based on the select line \( S_0 \).
    \item The second 2:1 MUX (MUX2) selects between \( I_2 \) and \( I_3 \) based on the same select line \( S_0 \).
    \item The outputs of MUX1 and MUX2 are then connected as inputs to the third 2:1 MUX (MUX3), which is controlled by the select line \( S_1 \).
\end{itemize}

Thus, the logic expression for the output \( Y \) can be written as:
\[
Y = \overline{S_1}\left(\overline{S_0}I_0 + S_0I_1\right) + S_1\left(\overline{S_0}I_2 + S_0I_3\right)
\]

This approach makes the circuit modular and simplifies the design process, allowing reuse of the 2:1 MUX cell.
When implemented using transmission gates, each 2:1 MUX consists of two transmission gates and one inverter. 
Hence, a 4:1 MUX can be realized using six transmission gates and three inverters.

This hierarchical design is highly efficient for CMOS-based implementation and forms the basis for larger multiplexing structures such as 8:1 or 16:1 MUXs.

