\section*{Design of 2:1 Multiplexer Using Transmission Gate}

A 2:1 multiplexer (MUX) is a fundamental digital switching circuit that selects one of two input signals and forwards it to a single output line based on a select signal. 
In CMOS design, this can be efficiently implemented using transmission gates instead of conventional logic gates, to achieve lower power consumption and higher speed.

A transmission gate acts as a bidirectional switch controlled by complementary signals \( S \) and \( \overline{S} \). 
For a 2:1 MUX, two transmission gates are used — one for each input.

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{mux2to1 using trans.png}
    \caption{ 2:1 Multiplexer Using Transmission Gate }
    \label{fig:mux_schematic}
\end{figure}







\begin{itemize}
    \item The first transmission gate connects input \( D_0 \) to the output when the select signal \( S = 0 \).
    \item The second transmission gate connects input \( D_1 \) to the output when the select signal \( S = 1 \).
\end{itemize}

Each transmission gate is controlled by complementary signals:
\[
\text{For TG1: Control = } \overline{S}, \quad \text{For TG2: Control = } S
\]

When \( S = 0 \), TG1 is ON and TG2 is OFF, so \( D_0 \) is passed to the output.
When \( S = 1 \), TG1 is OFF and TG2 is ON, so \( D_1 \) is passed to the output.

The Boolean equation governing the 2:1 MUX is:
\[
Y = \overline{S} \cdot D_0 + S \cdot D_1
\]

This implementation requires two transmission gates and one inverter (to generate \(\overline{S}\)).

The advantages of using transmission gates include:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Reduced transistor count compared to gate-based MUX design.
    \item Full voltage swing and minimal signal degradation.
    \item Symmetrical operation for logic ‘0’ and logic ‘1’.
\end{itemize}

Thus, the 2:1 MUX using transmission gates provides an area-efficient and power-efficient design suitable for VLSI applications.














